# Untitled

## Review Coiled Coils – A Model System for the 21st Century
## Trends
Coiled coils assemble into more complex structures than previously expected. In addition to the familiar fibers, they form barrels, sheets, funnels, and spirals. While many yield mechanically rigid structures and mediate oligomerization, an increasing number are involved in more-complex functions such as membrane fusion, the transduction of conformational changes, or the transport of other molecules.
## The Coiled Coil
In 1953, Francis Crick [1,2] and Linus Pauling [3] proposed independently of each other that a prominent group of proteins defined by William Astbury on the basis of fiber diffraction data (k-me-f, for keratin, myosin, epidermin, and fibrinogen [4]) would show a common structure formed by $\propto$ -helices running in the same direction and twisting around each other. Pauling referred to structures of this type as ‘compound helices’ and envisaged a range of stoichiometries and periodicities for them, remaining noncommittal about their side-chain packing interactions. By contrast, Crick, who called these structures ‘coiled coils’, placed side-chain interactions at the core of his model. He recognized that $\propto$ -helices, when twisted around each other at an angle of about $20^{\circ}$ , would interlock their side-chains systematically along the fiber, repeating the same interactions every seven residues (or two turns of the helix). He described this interlocking of residues as ‘knobs’ into ‘holes’, and deduced that coiled coils would show a sequence periodicity of seven residues (the heptad repeat[7_TD\$DIF] see Glossary), with hydrophobic residues in the first and fourth position forming the knobs-into-holes packing interactions[1_TD\$DIF] and providing the energy needed to distort the $\propto$ -helices. He offered a full parameterization of the proposed structure and showed how the path of the helical backbones could be computed by a simple set of equations. This model was immediately convincing and became the canonical account of coiled-coil structure, triggering the rapid development of a field dedicated to understanding $\propto$ -helical fibers.
The first sequences of coiled coils showed the anticipated heptad pattern [5,6], impressively confirming Crick's conjectures; they opened the path to a deeper understanding of $\propto$ -fibrous proteins through bioinformatic analysis and structure prediction, further enhanced by the first high-resolution structures of coiled coils [7,8], which confirmed the anticipated knobsinto-holes packing of side-chains and enabled template-directed modeling. These approaches led to detailed models relating sequence to structure in many fibrous proteins, such as tropomyosin [9], myosin [10], intermediate filaments [11], spectrins [12], prokaryotic surface proteins [13–15], and the Giardia cytoskeleton [16]. In the process, it became clear that many coiled coils deviate locally [10,17] or even globally [14–16] from heptad periodicity.
Many coiled coils show alterations to the canonical knobs-into-holes packing, for example through axial rotation of the constituent helices, various repeat patterns and periodicities, or interactions along multiple interfaces.
Because of our quantitative understanding of their structural principles, coiled coils are uniquely suited for the design of new forms with novel structures and rationally engineered functionality.
All this work was done on long fibers with clear sequence periodicities, but in 1986 Carolyn Cohen and David Parry argued in the pages of this journal (Trends in Biochemical Sciences, TIBS) that an understanding of coiled-coil interactions would be not only relevant for fibrous proteins but also for many, much more compact globular and membrane proteins [18]. In an analysis of high-resolution structures available at the time, they identified coiled-coil interactions in a diverse range of proteins, concluding that they could ‘be recognized as a common stabilizing structural motif in proteins of all types’. Their insight was borne out 1 year later with the highresolution structure of the ColE1 Rop protein, a homodimeric four-helix bundle [19], and became firmly established only 2 years later with the identification of a common dimerization element in eukaryotic transcription factors, the leucine zipper [20,21]. The helices of Rop and the leucine zipper only comprise about four heptads each, and marked a clear departure from the long fibers, extending over hundreds of residues, which had been the focus of coiled-coil studies up to that point. In particular, the leucine zipper turned out to be easily manipulated, integrated into heterologous constructs, and analyzed by high-resolution structural methods, providing an excellent model system for the analysis of sequence–structure relationships and for protein engineering applications; in the process it revolutionized coiled-coil studies. Whereas Rop and the leucine zipper had been the third and fourth high-resolution structure of a coiled coil to be determined, the following years saw an explosion of such structures, mostly of similarly short coiled coils, but increasingly also of longer proteins. Indeed, some longer fibers became tractable by fusing them in pieces to leucine-zipper adaptors and solving their structure in a divide-and-conquer approach [22], as we did a few years ago for the 100 nm fiber of a bacterial trimeric autotransporter [23].
A decade after Carolyn Cohen and David Parry had pointed out the widespread occurrence of knobs-into-holes interactions in proteins of the cytosol and the membrane, we set out – again in the pages of this journal – to describe the diversity of coiled-coil forms that had begun to emerge from structural studies [24]. The overview we presented included a two-page table with an annotated classification of all available coiled-coil structures, probably the last point at which such a printed table (rather than an online database [25]) was still reasonable. The table burst the format of the journal and we are still grateful to TIBS for printing it nevertheless and preserving its aspect as we had envisaged it. In this table we captured two-, three-, and four-helical coiled coils in parallel and antiparallel orientation, all showing rod-shaped structures of various lengths, and left-handed supercoils built of heptads or closely related periodicities. Today, 20 years later, the diversity of the coiled-coil bestiary has grown far beyond this (Figure 1); it contains structures of between two and more than 20 helices forming fibers, levers, tubes, funnels, sheets, spirals, and rings; it covers periodicities leading to both left-handed and right-handed supercoils (and to straight helical bundles in between); and includes local departures from $\propto$ -helical structure such as $3_{10}$ -helices, $\pi$ -turns, and even short $\upbeta$ -strands, as in the $\propto/\upbeta$ -coiled coils we have recently characterized [26]. We wish to outline here the structural principles of this diversity as they have emerged since our 1996 article, review the range of functions it enables, and argue that the versatility of this fold and our ability to manipulate it in quantitative ways will continue to increase its importance for protein science in the years to come.
## A Multitude of Forms
The architecture of a coiled coil is determined by two basic properties of its repeating sequence: the periodicity and the biophysical nature of the side-chains. In Crick's model [1,2] the periodicity is seven residues over two helical turns, resulting in 3.5 residues per turn $(7/2=3.5)$ ). If the seven positions are denoted $a{-}g$ , hydrophobic residues are mainly found in a and $d$ , and hydrophilic residues at all other positions. When plotted on a helical wheel (Figure 2A, first panel), this periodicity can be seen to place the hydrophobic positions on the same face of the helix, allowing the formation of a hydrophobic seam along the length of the structure. However, in an undistorted $\propto$ -helix with 3.63 residues per turn on average, this seam drifts by 0.13 residues
## Glossary
Core residue: a residue that projects into the helical bundle and engages in knobs-into-holes or knobs-to-knobs packing.
Crick equations: a set of parametric equations that describe the position of backbone atoms in $\propto$ -helical coiled coils.
Hendecad repeat: a sequence pattern similar to the heptad repeat, but with 11 residues per repeat. Positions one, four, five, and eight act as core residues. Packing involves both knobs-into-holes and knobs-toknobs interactions. Hendecads lead to coiled coils with very little supercoiling.
Heptad repeat: repeating sequence pattern of seven residues. Positions one and four are typically hydrophobic and act as core residues, all others are usually polar or charged. The heptad repeat causes left-handed supercoiling and is the only pattern compatible with continuous knobs-into-holes packing. Knobs-into-holes packing: a mode of protein interaction in which sidechains of two or more $\propto$ -helices interdigitate (pack) such that a sidechain (knob) from one helix is placed in a space surrounded by side-chains from one or more opposite helices (hole).
Knobs-to-knobs packing: packing mode that is found in combination with knobs-into-holes packing. In knobs-to-knobs packing, the sidechain of a core residue does not pack into a hole but against the sidechains of equivalent residues from opposite helices.
Pentadecad repeat: a sequence pattern with 15 residues per repeat. Positions one, four, five, eight, and 12 act as core residues. Packing involves both knobs-into-holes and knobs-to-knobs interactions. Pentadecads cause right-handed supercoiling to the same extent to which heptads cause left-handed supercoiling.
Supercoiling: continuous bending of $\propto$ -helices to allow them to interact in a regular fashion along a central axis. The need for bending arises from the discrepancy between the structural periodicity of an $\propto$ -helix (3.63 residues per helical turn) and the sequence periodicity of the underlying polypeptide chain (heptad, 3.5; hendecad, 3.67; pentadecad, 3.75,...).
(or $13^{\circ}$ ) per turn counterclockwise (here and in the following as seen from the N-terminus of the helix). To achieve regular packing, coiled-coil helices therefore bend (supercoil) to reduce their periodicity to 3.5 relative to a central axis – because $\propto$ -helices are right-handed, the required supercoil is left-handed. This allows their residues to occupy the same position every two turns of the helix relative to the central axis, and mesh in a regular fashion along the core, with residues in positions a and $d$ (knobs) packing into spaces surrounded by four side-chains of the facing helix (holes), next to the cognate residues in a’ and $d^{\prime}$ . As we will explain below, such regular core packing is only possible with heptad repeats; all other periodicities cause departures from knobs-into-holes geometry, which is probably the main reason why heptad periodicity is so dominant in nature.
Other periodicities are possible, however [27], as already envisaged by Linus Pauling in 1953 [3]. In essence, they all derive from insertions of three or four residues into an underlying heptad repeat (called stammers and stutters, respectively [17]). Three residues form somewhat less than a full helical turn, raise the drift of the hydrophobic seam locally to $30^{\circ}$ counterclockwise per turn, and tighten the left-handed supercoil; four residues form more than a turn, change the drift to $4^{\circ}$ clockwise, and relax the supercoil. These changes affect core packing in similar ways: Relative to the path of the core in a heptad repeat, stammers cause a local change of $-17^{\circ}$ , requiring a compensatory shift in residue positions that corresponds to a clockwise rotation of the helical wheel diagram: position $d$ moves to the center of the core, a outward and $g$ inward to yield an $a{-}d{-}g$ core (Figure 2B, first panel). Stutters cause a change of $+17^{\circ}$ , requiring a counterclockwise compensatory shift: position a moves to the center, $d$ outward, and $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{e}}$ inward to yield an a– $\cdot d\cdot$ –e core (Figure 2B, second panel). In both cases, the compensation means that one position now points directly towards the central axis (x geometry) and two positions point sideways, enclosing a central cavity (da geometry), an arrangement we call knobs-to-knobs packing [27]. When stutters and stammers do not occur merely as local discontinuities, but are repeated regularly along the helix, they lead to coiled coils that deviate globally from the Crick model. In particular, stutters and heptads may combine to yield a range of periodicities, such as hendecad repeats $(7+4=11$ ; Figure 2A, second panel) and pentadecad repeats $(7+4+4$ $=15$ ; Figure 2A, third panel); by contrast, stammers and periodicities derived from them are rare in natural coiled coils. More generally, all periodicities accessible to coiled coils without disruption of the helices can be described as combinations of 3- and 4-residue segments that start with a core residue [27,28]; alternating elements of 3 and 4 yield knobs-into-holes packing, and consecutive elements of the same type (3–3 or 4–4) yield knobs-to-knobs interactions. The supercoil of these coiled coils is determined by the difference of their periodicity to the 3.63 of an undistorted $\propto$ -helix: coiled coils with periodicities of 3.5 (7/2, heptads) are left-handed, with 3.67 (11/3, hendecads) being essentially straight, and 3.75 (15/4, pentadecads) being right-handed (Figure 3).
The periodicities observed in coiled coils are limited by the strain they impose on the helices. The further a periodicity is from 3.63, the more pronounced the supercoil needed to compensate for it. This is the reason why stammers are rare in coiled coils: periodicities below 3.4 approach the limits of $\propto$ -helical backbone conformations. Indeed, two structures of stammers in heptad coiled coils show that these are accommodated by local transitions to $3_{10}$ -helical structure (Figure 3) [26,29]. We therefore asked ourselves what would happen if we introduced $2\times3$ or even $3\times3$ residues into a heptad background; we found that the strain breaks the helices into short $\upbeta$ - strands to yield a completely new coiled-coil variant, the $\propto/\upbeta$ -coiled coil (Figure 3) [26].
Whereas periodicity dominates the backbone conformations of coiled coils, their oligomeric state and helix orientation are mainly determined by the nature of the interface residues. In parallel coiled coils, for example, the combination of $\upbeta$ -branched residues in position a (Ile, Val) and $\gamma$ -branched residues in position $d$ (Leu) favors dimers, and the reverse distribution favors
