Positions e and $g$ flanking the core are equally important in determining oligomeric state and helix orientation. In coiled coils formed of two or three helices, these positions usually contain many charged residues, which provide specific interactions through salt bridges and shield the hydrophobic core from solvent. When one of these positions becomes non-polar through the inclusion of many hydrophobic residues, the resulting broader hydrophobic surface favors tetramers, usually in antiparallel orientation. This broader interface formed by three positions can be seen as having two seams of core residues that share one position $(g-d$ and $d{-}a$ , or $d{-}a$ and a–e) [33,34]. Axially-symmetric packing of such a larger interface causes the shared position to point towards the central axis $(x$ geometry) and the two other positions to point sideways, enclosing a central cavity (da geometry). We have already encountered this packing in the context of stutters and stammers; however, here it is not induced by a change in periodicity, but rather by the inclusion of a third hydrophobic position into the packing, which could be rationalized as causing a global axial rotation of the helices by about $26^{\circ}$ relative to the central axis (Figures 2B and 4). The preference for tetrameric structure results from the ability to shield a broader hydrophobic core, and the preference for an antiparallel orientation results from the advantage of combining the $x$ positions in one pair of diagonally-opposite helices with the da positions in the other (termed complementary $\pmb{x}$ –da packing [35]).
Further broadening of the interface leads to even higher oligomeric states (Figure 4). Inclusion of a fourth position yields two adjacent seams $(g-d$ and a–e) and allows the formation of pentamers, hexamers, and heptamers [36,37]; their axially-symmetric packing marks a return to the helix rotation state of canonical coiled coils. Inclusion of a fifth position yields two seams separated by an intervening position $(g-d$ and $e{\mathrm{-}}b$ , separated by $a$ ; or $c{-}g$ and a–e, separated by d) and allows for even higher oligomers, the largest currently known being the 12-helical barrel of the multidrug efflux pore TolC [38]. The symmetric packing of such helices with five interface positions involves the same axial rotation relative to the canonical state as in the case with three interface positions above.
## Trends in Biochemical Sciences
Coiled-coil helices with broader interfaces have been called ‘bifaceted’, and the possible three types have been denoted I (seams sharing a common position), II (adjacent seams), and III (seams separated by one position) [33,34]; Figure 4 shows the three types, compared to a canonical interface (type N). As their seams move apart, bifaceted helices produce structures that range from helical bundles, over tubes with increasingly large solvent-filled pores, to fully-open sheets.
