## Structure [1_TD\$DIF]As a Basis for Functional Diversity
The diverse structures formed by coiled coils support a range of biological functions, based on several fundamental properties. One of these is stability. Coiled coils may well represent the most stable protein fold known to us as a result of the regularity of their packing interactions. For example, the stalk of the archaeal surface-layer protein tetrabrachion survives heating to $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $6{\sf M}$ guanidinium hydrochloride and is only denatured by $70\%$ sulfuric acid [39]. While such extreme stability is unusual even for a coiled coil, we note that much shorter coiled coils of only four or five heptads, such as variants of the leucine zipper [30] or designed proteins [37], often resist high temperatures even in the presence of denaturants. Correspondingly, the fibers that originally laid the basis for coiled-coil studies all mediate mechanical strength; they are found in biological materials, such as hair, nails, horns, and blood clots, as well as in structural elements of cells and tissues, such as muscle fibers, the cytoskeleton, or the extracellular matrix. With the rise of molecular biology, coiled coils have increasingly also been identified (i) at the cell surface, as domains of proteins forming pili and flagella, surface layers, adhesins, receptors, and spacers between the inner and outer membrane of bacteria; (ii) as arms and levers of molecular machines, such as chaperones (Figure 1J,K) [40], serving to grip and/or move macromolecular substrates; and (iii) as reliable mediators of oligomerization, even at very short lengths (as seen in a range of transcription factors [20,24], e.g., in Figure 1B).
The biological functions resulting from coiled-coil stability have been known and documented for decades, but over the past 20 years two important new areas of functionality have emerged in membrane fusion [41,42] and transmembrane signal transduction [35,43,44]. These are based on a second fundamental property of coiled coils, namely a flat energy landscape with many, nearly isoenergetic, states which allow the evolution of coiled coils that can rearrange their structure dynamically in response to changes in their environment. The closeness of different states in the structure-space of coiled coils is documented nowhere better than in the many variants of the GCN4 leucine zipper [30,45–50], for which dimers, trimers, tetramers, and heptamers have been obtained in parallel and antiparallel orientation, staggered and unstaggered, through minor changes in the sequence; some of these variants can even be switched between forms by environmental conditions [45,50]. The potential for conformational change in molecules mediating membrane fusion was illustrated impressively with the low-pH structure of influenza hemagglutinin, which undergoes a large-scale rearrangement from neutral pH [51,52]; this rearrangement, driven by the refolding of a long loop region into a trimeric coiled-coil stem, projects the fusion peptide from close to the viral membrane into the host membrane, $100\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ away. The transmission of molecular signals across membranes does not involve any such major refolding, but instead relies on the energetic closeness between canonical packing and type I bifaceted packing in four-helical bundles. As shown for the HAMP domain in two-component signal transduction receptors (Figure 1G,H) [35,53], axial helix rotation, in other words the transition between canonical knobs-into-holes and complementary x–da packing through the concerted rotation of all four helices, is an attractive mechanism for the long-range transmission of ligand-induced conformational change across large distances [43,44].
There is a further area of functionality for coiled coils, which has grown rapidly in importance in recent years: solute transport. This is based on the ability of bifaceted coiled-coil helices to assemble into barrel-shaped complexes with pores of considerable diversity. At the first structure determination of a coiled coil with more than four helices (the pentameric oligomerization domain of cartilage oligomeric matrix protein), the conspicuous axial pore was already recognized as a viable model for pentameric transmembrane ion channels [54]. Work in this direction was stalled, however, by the difficulties of working with membrane proteins (particularly of determining their high-resolution structures as a prerequisite for understanding them at the atomic level) and by the challenges of designing synthetic proteins as model systems for such channels. Substantial progress only became possible a few years ago [36,55] once the design protocols had been developed [37,56,57]. Most impressive among these efforts is the design of a zinc/proton antiporter, Rocker (Figure 5A) [55], which translocates three to four protons in one direction for every $Z n^{2+}$ ion in the other. Rocker is a homotetrameric, antiparallel, membrane-embedded coiled coil with two ion binding sites close to its ends. By design, it oscillates dynamically between two states, binding $Z n^{2+}$ at one site when it releases it at the other. Because the binding sites can either coordinate zinc ions or protons, but not both, the design ensures that these ions are transported in opposite directions. A further exciting development for structures built of bifaceted coiled coils are soluble enzyme barrels, whose central pores are configured to catalyze chemical reactions (Figure 5B) [58,59]. A recently presented construct of seven parallel helices, each carrying a Cys-His-Glu catalytic triad, is capable of hydrolyzing $p$ -nitrophenyl acetate with rates approaching one thousandth of the rate achieved by natural proteins, offering an attractive starting point for the design of new biocatalysts.
## Outlook
As we have outlined in this article, coiled coils use a small number of quantifiable principles to achieve a large structural and functional diversity. The ability to reproduce these principles by design has led to substantial advances in emulating biological functions, not achievable by other de novo approaches. Because natural proteins mediating biological functions rarely show the high degree of symmetry seen in coiled coil-based designs so far, an important challenge will be to design asymmetries into future constructs, allowing more-differentiated control of the functional output (see Outstanding Questions). As the only fold computable by parametric equations, coiled coils are uniquely positioned to enable this step.
